<p>These are the desk’s publishing rules. They are our own standards, not legal advice and not a promise about any particular dispute. The <a href="{{ '/disclaimer/' | url }}">disclaimer</a> says what the reporting is not. <a href="{{ '/corrections/' | url }}">Corrections</a> says how to fix a fact.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<p>Every factual claim in a published article cites a named, linkable source in the sentence it supports. The source should be a primary document or a reputable publisher: an agency, a regulator, a manufacturer, a court, or the outlet that reported the fact. The sources list at the bottom repeats those pages with a title, a URL, a publisher, and a date.</p>
<p>We do not invent quotes, numbers, prices, dates, or events. If a figure is not in a source, it stays out. Wording is our own. A quote from a source is brief, and it is attributed.</p>
<p>Company announcements and press releases are reported as the company’s own statements (“X says,” “according to X”). A claim counts as independently verified only when a source other than the company confirms it. Articles say which is which.</p>
<h2>People and companies</h2>
<p>We do not state or imply that a named person or company did something wrong unless an authoritative source says so. When that source says so, the article attributes it, with words such as “according to” or “alleged,” and it links the source. We do not speculate about a person’s motive, health, finances, or private conduct. When a passage is in doubt, we leave it out. A story that alleges wrongdoing by a named person or company stays off the public site until that copy has been reviewed.</p>
<h2>Opinion, analysis, and speculation</h2>
<p>News is sourced fact. Opinion, analysis, and speculation are labeled at the top of the article. A label is not a substitute for the sources under the facts inside the piece.</p>
<h2>Images</h2>
<p>Images on the site are AI-generated photorealistic illustrations of the subject. They do not depict real identifiable people or actual news events, and they do not carry a visible AI caption. That disclosure is the <a href="{{ '/disclaimer/' | url }}">disclaimer</a> section “AI-generated imagery.” The alt text describes the picture. A third-party press photo is separately credited and kept small, and it needs a documented permission source as well as a licence. Attribution and fair use do not clear that requirement. It is not a stock-agency file. Icons and other interface graphics are not article photographs.</p>
<h2>Advice</h2>
<p>Articles are not health, legal, or financial advice, and they are not instructions for a repair or an investment. Nothing on the site is a guarantee of an outcome. Where a story touches health, law, or money, it carries a short disclaimer and still cites the underlying source. Confirm anything that matters with that source or with a qualified professional.</p>
<h2>Corrections and contact</h2>
<p>A published article shows a <strong>Last updated</strong> date. That date is <code>updatedAt</code> in the file, and it is <code>dateModified</code> in the page’s structured data. Send a correction through the <a href="{{ '/contact/' | url }}">contact page</a>. We do not publish a street address.</p>
